.SH "Detailed Description"
.PP 
The connectivity defines the coarse topology of the forest\&. 

A 3D forest consists of one or more octrees, each of which a logical cube\&. Each tree has a local coordinate system, which defines the origin and the direction of its x-, y-, and z-axes as well as the numbering of its faces, edges, and corners\&. Each tree may connect to any other tree (including itself) across any of its faces, edges and/or corners, where the neighbor may be arbitrarily rotated and/or flipped\&. The \fBp8est_connectivity\fP data structure stores these connections\&.
.PP
We impose the following requirement for consistency of \fBp8est_balance\fP :
.PP
\fBNote\fP
.RS 4
If a connectivity implies natural connections between trees that are edge neighbors without being face neighbors, these edges shall be encoded explicitly in the connectivity\&. If a connectivity implies natural connections between trees that are corner neighbors without being edge or face neighbors, these corners shall be encoded explicitly in the connectivity\&. Please see the documentation of \fBp8est_connectivity_t\fP for the exact encoding convention\&.
.RE
.PP
We provide various predefined connectivities by dedicated constructors, such as
.PP
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fBp8est_connectivity_new_unitcube\fP for the unit square,
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fBp8est_connectivity_new_periodic\fP for the periodic unit square,
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fBp8est_connectivity_new_brick\fP for a rectangular grid of trees,
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fBp8est_connectivity_new_drop\fP for a sparsely loop of trees\&. 
.PP

.SH "Macro Definition Documentation"
.PP 
